✅ Yes, you can definitely create a custom mutable class in Java.
Mutable means you can change the internal state (fields) of the object after it's created — just like StringBuilder.

📦 Example: Custom Mutable Class
java
Copy
Edit
public class Person {
    private String name;

    public Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    // Setter (allows change)
    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    // Getter
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}
✅ Usage:
java
Copy
Edit
Person p = new Person("Talha");
System.out.println(p.getName());  // Output: Talha

p.setName("Ahmed");
System.out.println(p.getName());  // Output: Ahmed
🔐 To Make a Class Immutable Instead:
Make fields private final

No setters

Only getters

Initialize fields via constructor only

